
What are the columns in your datasets you have seen?

In my last project, I worked on a **Customer Data Management (CDM)** platform that integrated data from multiple source systems to create a single golden record for our customers and related entities. We used **Databricks** for our data pipelines and **Reltio** as our Master Data Management (MDM) hub.

The main datasets I worked with were **Customer, Vehicle, Organization, and Location**.

The **Customer** dataset was our core entity. Beyond standard fields like **FirstName** and **LastName**, it had a rich structure. For example, contact information like **Email** and **Phone** was stored as nested objects, where each entry had its own type (such as **Home** or **Work**) and a **ValidationStatus**. We also tracked customer privacy choices in a **PrivacyPreferences** object with flags like **DoNotMarket**, which was critical for compliance.

The **Vehicle** dataset was keyed by the **VIN**. It contained descriptive attributes like **Model** and **Year**, financial data such as **MSRPAmount**, and various status flags like **PreownedIndicator**.

We also had an **Organization** dataset for our business customers, which included **LegalName**, **DUNSNumber** for identification, and industry classification codes.

Finally, to ensure consistency, all physical addresses were standardized into a central **Location** dataset. This was more than just **AddressLine1** and **City**; it also stored **VerificationStatus** from our address validation service and **GeoLocation** coordinates.

This structured approach ensured our data was clean, standardized, and ready for use by downstream analytics and operational systems.
